  "processing": "Detailed atlas first-level geometry supplies the physical land. Rough historical coasts are discarded in a coastal band; nearest inland ownership extends over canonical land within the same connected landmass. Detached islands without an inland core use retained ownership. The Sakhalin 50th-parallel frontier is preserved through the band. The source’s artificial 180-degree cut is resolved onto the connected Chukotka mainland when the retained extent reaches that cut. Exact atlas paths are stored as source-file, ring and vertex-run references. Only political lines and intersection vertices are stored locally. The world overview classifies physical coast versus inland frontier; its coordinates never enter the historical mesh. One topology resolves overlaps, and each date only changes ownership and color. 1478 uses the post-annexation 1482 source; 1485 uses 1487. The 1648 Pacific milestone uses the generalized 1653 source. Finland from 1809 reuses the existing Finnish land to repair omissions at the rough source coast; historical eastern extensions remain from Cliopatria. The 1812 extent unions the 1811 and 1812 sources to avoid treating French occupation of Moscow as a sovereign cession. 1867 removes Alaska from a source interval that ends in that year. The 1914 and 1917 references use the pre-war 1913 source. Protectorates reuse their last independent source outline and remain separately identified. 1922 uses the 1924 external outline after the Far Eastern Republic joined; Bukhara and Khorezm remain separately identified allied republics. The 1945 post-war reference uses the 1953 source to exclude occupation zones in Poland and Germany; small 1945–1953 frontier adjustments are generalized. Late Soviet frames reuse the original republic ownership and inland paths, resolved onto the same detailed physical land as the imperial frames. 1991 relabels the same republic reference as independent successor states.",
